WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.148 --> 00:00:09.809 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Europe episode one of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:21.223 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We begin with seven places around the North Atlantic and northern Europe:
Iceland, Ireland, the United Kingdom, Denmark, Norway, Sweden and Finland.

3
00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:26.328 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Iceland and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:29.331 --> 00:00:40.802 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Iceland stands apart in the North Atlantic, northwest of the United Kingdom
and west of Norway. It is an island country, not part of Scandinavia’s
mainland.

5
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:53.019 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Althing assembly began at Thingvellir in the year 930. Imagine people
gathering outdoors in this volcanic landscape to make laws and settle
disputes.

6
00:00:53.670 --> 00:01:05.698 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Iceland as our distant northwestern island: old parliament, volcanic
ground, and a wide stretch of Atlantic before the European mainland.

7
00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:10.894 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ireland and its neighbours into a closer view.

8
00:01:17.319 --> 00:01:27.896 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ireland is the island west of Great Britain. The independent country covers
most of it; Northern Ireland, in the northeast, belongs to the United Kingdom.

9
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:41.684 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ireland’s national emblem is a harp. You can find it on Irish coins and
official symbols: a musical instrument gives this country a memorable visual
signature.

10
00:01:42.344 --> 00:01:52.712 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the harp on the western island. Keep Ireland separate from Northern
Ireland, and look east across the Irish Sea toward Great Britain.

11
00:01:56.152 --> 00:02:07.240 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The United Kingdom includes England, Scotland and Wales on Great Britain, plus
Northern Ireland across the water. Ireland is a separate neighbouring country.

12
00:02:07.896 --> 00:02:22.989 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The United Kingdom’s constitution is not collected in a single written
document. It draws on laws, court decisions and conventions: a different
arrangement from countries with one codified constitutional text.

13
00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:33.143 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Use the island outlines: Ireland on the west, Great Britain on the east, with
Northern Ireland completing the United Kingdom across the Irish Sea.

14
00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:37.384 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Denmark and its neighbours into a closer view.

15
00:02:43.339 --> 00:02:55.750 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Denmark sits above Germany, between the North Sea and the Baltic. Its Jutland
peninsula reaches north from Germany, with major islands extending east toward
Sweden.

16
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:09.275 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Danish realm also includes Greenland and the Faroe Islands, both with
self-government. This close European view shows Denmark’s mainland and nearby
islands, not the whole realm.

17
00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:18.871 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Denmark as the low bridge between Germany and Scandinavia: a
peninsula pointing north and islands stepping east toward Sweden.

18
00:03:20.552 --> 00:03:23.664 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Norway and its neighbours into a closer view.

19
00:03:31.194 --> 00:03:43.268 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Norway follows the western side of the Scandinavian Peninsula. Its long coast
faces the North Atlantic, while Sweden lies along much of its eastern border.

20
00:03:43.926 --> 00:03:56.291 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Norway’s fjords are sea-filled valleys shaped by glaciers. The western fjords
recognised by UNESCO show steep rock walls and deep water reaching far inland.

21
00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:09.815 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a long, deeply notched western coast. Norway is the Atlantic-facing
side of the peninsula; Sweden is the broader neighbour to its east.

22
00:04:14.203 --> 00:04:26.312 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Sweden occupies the eastern side of the Scandinavian Peninsula, between Norway
and the Baltic Sea. Finland lies across the northern border and the Gulf of
Bothnia.

23
00:04:26.972 --> 00:04:40.707 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
On September third, 1967, Sweden changed from driving on the left to driving
on the right. Picture a whole country carefully changing sides in one morning.

24
00:04:41.397 --> 00:04:52.403 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Norway on the Atlantic side and Sweden on the Baltic side. The traffic
switch is Sweden’s extra memory hook, attached to the eastern half of the
peninsula.

25
00:04:57.106 --> 00:05:06.916 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Finland lies east of Sweden and west of Russia, with the Gulf of Finland along
its southern edge. Estonia is across that gulf, farther south.

26
00:05:07.572 --> 00:05:19.472 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Sauna culture is part of Finland’s UNESCO-listed living heritage. It is about
family traditions, shared spaces and wellbeing, rather than simply taking a
hot bath.

27
00:05:20.137 --> 00:05:30.632 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Finland as the eastern Nordic country: Sweden to the west, Russia to
the east, and sauna culture as the fact that fixes its position in memory.

28
00:05:44.303 --> 00:05:55.344 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Retrace the islands first: Iceland far northwest, Ireland west of Great
Britain, and the United Kingdom spanning Great Britain and Northern Ireland.

29
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:13.784 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Then Denmark below the Scandinavian Peninsula, Norway along its Atlantic side,
Sweden on its Baltic side, and Finland farther east. Connect each location
with its fact, and try tracing the route without the labels.
